Use [[https://firmware-selector.openwrt.org/?target=ipq806x%2Fgeneric&id=netgear_r7800|https://firmware-selector.openwrt.org]] to download OpenWrt firmware images and optionally generate a customized OpenWrt image with different installed packages. For an explanation of different image types, see [[docs:guide-user:installation:before.installation|FAQ before installation]].

If your router is running stock Netgear firmware, then you can use the Netgear stock firmware's built-in firmware updater to install OpenWrt, without needing to use TFTP. Installing using TFTP is a fallback option, which you can use if you are unable to install using a web browser. 

//The instructions below were tested on stock Netgear firmware V1.0.2.92 and successfully installed OpenWrt 23.05 // 

  - Connect your computer to the Wi-Fi router's LAN port using a wired ethernet cable.
  - Open a web browser and login to Netgear router's admin page (default is http://192.168.1.1). Under **Advanced** page, select **Administration** -> **Firmware Update**.
  - On the **Firmware Update** page, select and upload the OpenWrt ''-factory.img'' file. Select **Yes** button to continue install. Wait a few minutes for the router to reboot.
  - After the power LED stops blinking and stays solidly lit white, open a web browser and navigate to http://192.168.1.1 address
  - Ignore **Your connection is not private** warning message in the web browser, and continue by selecting **Advanced** button, then select the **Proceed to 192.168.1.1 (unsafe)** link.
  - Login to OpenWrt with the default OpenWrt username ''root'' and no password. 
(Note: there is no need to flash "sysupgrade" file, as there are no functional differences between factory and sysupgrade images.)

The information below is kept for archival purposes for upgrading from OpenWrt versions prior to 18.06.0-rc1.

  * Master, 19.07 and 18.06 have since Q2/2018 a larger kernel partition, so that kernel 4.14+ and flow offloading is possible. Additionally master, 19.07 and 18.06 currently use 70 MB larger area on flash, so installing more packages is possible. That change was made for master and 18.06 branches in June 2018. 
  * Due to that, you will need TFTP flash once to upgrade/downgrade from the older builds (17.01.x or 18.06.0-rc1 or earlier). Sysupgrade works normally after you have upgraded to 18.06.0 or newer. 
  * See TFTP flashing instructions below, originally from forum: [[https://forum.openwrt.org/t/netgear-r7800-exploration-ipq8065-qca9984/285/5?u=tmomas|R7800 TFTP installation instructions in Openwrt forum ]]

===== Broken NAND Chips/Blocks =====

There are some models, which have broken NANDs. when flashing the Kernel, some (mostly one) blocks are skipped. As a result, the start of the root-partition (called "ubi" in this model) comes later.

**Result: Bootloop of the device**

What you can do is here, edit the file ''target/linux/ipq806x/files-x.x/arch/arm/boot/dts/qcom-ipq8065-r7800.dts'' and put the startAddress for "ubi" up. 
''(&nand_controller --> nand@0 --> partitions --> ubi@1880000)'', currently(04.2020) at line 360  )

Edit the two numbers showing **1880000 to 18A0000**. (skip 2 blocks)

Then compile the image using make.
===== Debugging - kernel crash logs =====

Since late February 2022, the R7800 .DTS in OpenWrt master (but not in 21.02 or earlier) has the needed definitions for the RAMoops pstore kernel crash logs, the functionality for which has been packaged as kmod-ramoops in February 2022.

The kernel crashes are logged to 'ramoops' pstore files into RAM in /sys/fs/pstore . These files in RAM will survive a warm reboot (like in connection with a crash), but will not survive a cold reboot (power-off).

The feature can be enabled by selecting the kmod-ramoops to the build, or installing kmod-ramoops via opkg. 

